== Decision / utility audit ==

[[Decision theory]] preserves normative versus descriptive scope. [[Utility (decision theory)]] is treated as a representation of preference/value under a model, not as money, pleasure, or objective social welfare by definition.

== Game / mechanism audit ==

[[Game theory]] is broader than zero-sum conflict and equilibrium is not presented as efficient or fair by default. [[Mechanism design]] reverses the usual analysis direction by asking what rules induce desired outcomes, while preserving feasibility and incentive tradeoffs.

== Coordination / collective-action audit ==

[[Coordination problem]] is separated from [[Collective action]]. Coordination can fail from equilibrium-selection or information problems without an incentive to defect; collective-action problems can arise because individually attractive behavior undermines a jointly preferred outcome.

== Public-good / commons audit ==

[[Public good]] uses the non-rival/non-excludable framework and explicitly rejects the government-provision alias. [[Commons]] incorporates Ostrom's institutional correction: common resources are not defined by inevitable tragedy.

== Delegation / governance audit ==

[[Principal–agent problem]] separates hidden action/moral hazard from hidden information/adverse selection. [[Governance]] is broader than government and includes formal/informal institutions, decision rights, implementation, monitoring, conflict resolution, accountability, and amendment.

== Reputation / Sybil audit ==

[[Reputation system]] treats scores as model outputs contingent on identity and aggregation assumptions. [[Sybil attack]] requires one actor to exploit multiple identities as if they were independent; pseudonymity or multiple accounts alone is not sufficient.

== Cross-wave audit ==

Wave 10 connects cleanly to earlier canonical concepts:

* decision theory uses [[Probability]], [[Conditional probability]], [[Uncertainty]], and [[Causality]] without redefining them;
* strategic concepts connect to [[Intelligent agent]] and [[Multi-agent system]] without making computational agents the definition of game theory;
* institutional/collective concepts connect to [[Network]], [[Complex system]], [[Feedback]], and [[Adaptation (systems)]] without reducing governance to systems jargon;
* reputation/Sybil concepts depend on [[Identity]], [[Verification]], [[Computer security]], and [[Distributed system]] while remaining distinct identities.

No local crypto/project architecture defines a public Wave 10 concept.

